<section class="hot-chapter hot-wrap hot-narrow" id="before">
  <p class="hot-eyebrow">Before you order</p>
  <h2 style="margin:8px 0 28px">The things people ask before their first box</h2>
  <div class="hot-faq">
    <details><summary>Is it fresh or frozen?</summary><p class="hot-a">Frozen, on purpose. Our fish is blast-frozen to −10°F at harvest and vacuum sealed, so you can fill the freezer in November and cook across the whole season. <a href="/pages/seafood-freshness-omega-3-science">Why we freeze it.</a></p></details>
    <details><summary>How does it arrive?</summary><p class="hot-a">Insulated and frozen, with a frozen delivery guarantee; the published terms are on our <a href="/policies/shipping-policy">shipping policy</a> page. Open the box and move everything to the freezer as soon as it lands. The policy asks you to inspect it within 90 minutes of delivery.</p></details>
    <details><summary>When do I need to order for a holiday table?</summary><p class="hot-a">Orders ship on Tuesday or Wednesday and usually arrive Wednesday or Thursday, and delivery is not guaranteed for an exact day. So order at least a week before the table you are planning; the full terms are on our <a href="/policies/shipping-policy">shipping policy</a> page. The simplest plan is the one on this page: fill the freezer early, then cook across the season.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How long can I keep it in the freezer?</summary><p class="hot-a">Keep it sealed and at a steady freezer temperature, and do not refreeze anything you have already thawed. Each product page carries its own storage notes; the roe and caviar tins, for example, keep 2 months frozen unopened.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I need to thaw it first?</summary><p class="hot-a">Yes, for every recipe here. Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack is best, about 12 hours, then pat it dry. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature, and never in warm water.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How many people does each item feed?</summary><p class="hot-a">It is on every product page. As a guide for these menus: a steelhead loin and a whole trout each serve two, a 16 oz bag of shrimp serves four, branzino comes as two 6 oz fillets, and a 30 g tin of salmon roe finishes a plate for four.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the testing actually cover?</summary><p class="hot-a">Every supplier is third-party tested for nutrition, mercury and microplastics, then randomly audited to ensure consistent compliance with the Seatopia Standard. <a href="/pages/why-clean-seafood-matters">How we source it.</a></p></details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="hot-solstice" id="solstice">
  <div class="hot-wrap hot-cols">
    <div>
      <p class="hot-eyebrow">Monday, December 21 · The longest night</p>
      <h2>A ritual for the solstice</h2>
      <p>The solstice is the turning point of the year: the shortest day, the longest night, and from here the light comes back a minute at a time. It falls between the Hanukkah candles and Christmas Eve, two days before the full moon, and it is the moment we actually mark.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>Before the meal, step outside at dusk and watch the light go. Bring something back in: a branch, a stone, a piece of kindling. Light the candles from one match. Before anyone sits, take a breath and look at the people at the table. No toast required. Then put the board down, and let the long night be long.</p>
      <p class="hot-btns"><a class="hot-btn hot-btn-light" href="#nye">Plan the board</a><a class="hot-btn hot-btn-ghost" style="color:#F7EFE6;border-color:#F7EFE6" href="#freezer">Fill the freezer</a></p>
    </div>
